WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.305
This is the technical deep dive
for Book 10 of the Federal

3
00:00:04.305 --> 00:00:07.924
Modernization Workbook, Cloud
Boundary and Landing Zone.

4
00:00:07.924 --> 00:00:11.854
It's for the cloud and platform
engineers who build the landing zone,

5
00:00:11.854 --> 00:00:14.450
and the ISSOs who have to
evidence what's in it.

6
00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.674
The whole book comes down to one idea:

7
00:00:16.674 --> 00:00:20.975
the license says what you bought, but
the boundary says what you may use.

8
00:00:20.975 --> 00:00:25.063
Part 1 is about the boundary itself
and what it quietly takes away.

9
00:00:25.063 --> 00:00:27.709
Part 2 is about building
it into the landing

10
00:00:27.709 --> 00:00:30.650
zone and owning it in the
authorization package.

11
00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.663
The code stays in the chapters.

12
00:00:32.663 --> 00:00:35.120
Let's start with why this bites so late.

13
00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:39.895
Here's the trap.

14
00:00:39.895 --> 00:00:43.845
Two agencies can hold the same
license and get different products.

15
00:00:43.845 --> 00:00:47.285
The commercial tenant sees
network scores per location,

16
00:00:47.285 --> 00:00:50.433
smart routing for its SD-WAN,
and adoption reporting.

17
00:00:50.433 --> 00:00:52.370
The GCC tenant sees none of it,

18
00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:55.920
and nothing in the license
or the price list warns you.

19
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.428
Whether a feature is usable
depends on three checks lining up:

20
00:00:59.428 --> 00:01:03.625
what FedRAMP certified, what the
provider leaves out of your environment,

21
00:01:03.625 --> 00:01:06.445
and what your own authorizing
official accepted.

22
00:01:06.445 --> 00:01:11.133
Teams usually find the gap during an
authorization cycle, when it's expensive.

23
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:14.882
So the rule is simple:
no citation, no feature.

24
00:01:14.882 --> 00:01:16.990
Let's look at those three checks.

25
00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.952
Module 1 builds the foundation.

26
00:01:22.952 --> 00:01:26.103
First, the three checks
and who owns each one.

27
00:01:26.103 --> 00:01:28.054
Then why the gaps stay invisible,

28
00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:31.740
including a popular example that
turns out to be mostly wrong.

29
00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:36.810
Then what FedRAMP's 2026 rules actually
changed about the word boundary.

30
00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:42.814
Each check answers a different question,

31
00:01:42.814 --> 00:01:45.135
and none of them can
stand in for the others.

32
00:01:45.135 --> 00:01:47.435
The Marketplace tells
you what's certified,

33
00:01:47.435 --> 00:01:50.310
but not which features your
environment switches off.

34
00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.782
The provider's notes
tell you about features,

35
00:01:52.782 --> 00:01:55.922
but not whether your agency
authorized the integration.

36
00:01:55.922 --> 00:01:58.197
And your own package covers your choices,

37
00:01:58.197 --> 00:02:00.672
but nothing outside
the certified offering.

38
00:02:00.672 --> 00:02:01.836
Two highpoints.

39
00:02:01.836 --> 00:02:06.320
Agents you install on agency machines
are outside FedRAMP entirely,

40
00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:07.973
so they're yours to assess.

41
00:02:07.973 --> 00:02:11.506
And impact level and
features are different axes:

42
00:02:11.506 --> 00:02:15.384
the features missing in GCC
are missing in GCC High too.

43
00:02:15.384 --> 00:02:17.770
Next, why nobody notices.

44
00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:22.970
Why don't teams see it coming?

45
00:02:22.970 --> 00:02:27.805
The consoles are shared, each exclusion
hides in one sentence on its own page,

46
00:02:27.805 --> 00:02:32.870
features change underneath you, and a
missing signal just looks like a quiet day.

47
00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:35.345
But the correction matters just as much.

48
00:02:35.345 --> 00:02:40.245
A widely repeated example listed
five features as missing from GCC.

49
00:02:40.245 --> 00:02:44.357
Checked against the documentation,
only one actually is.

50
00:02:44.357 --> 00:02:48.707
The rest are GCC High gaps,
or belong to Azure entirely.

51
00:02:48.707 --> 00:02:52.908
An unverified story sends you to
build compensations you don't need.

52
00:02:52.908 --> 00:02:55.408
So only vendor-confirmed rows count.

53
00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:59.350
Now, a way to describe what
changes across any boundary.

54
00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:08.165
The program's model says a boundary is any
place where one of four things changes:

55
00:03:08.165 --> 00:03:13.077
which signals arrive, who an identity
resolves to, who has to enforce a control,

56
00:03:13.077 --> 00:03:16.838
and whether a claim is still
authoritative or only derived.

57
00:03:16.838 --> 00:03:21.874
That turns one boundary into one coherent
story instead of a pile of findings.

58
00:03:21.874 --> 00:03:26.372
It also matters because FedRAMP's
definitions exclude provider telemetry

59
00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:30.512
from federal customer data, so no
certification promises you a signal.

60
00:03:30.512 --> 00:03:33.731
The answer is design
guidance: probe what works,

61
00:03:33.731 --> 00:03:37.775
label substitutes as derived,
and cite every row with a date.

62
00:03:37.775 --> 00:03:40.260
Next, FedRAMP's new rules.

63
00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:49.247
FedRAMP changed its vocabulary in
2026, and the change is useful.

64
00:03:49.247 --> 00:03:53.355
Cloud services are now certified,
in Classes A through D,

65
00:03:53.355 --> 00:03:56.659
and Class C covers the
old Moderate baseline.

66
00:03:56.659 --> 00:04:01.511
The provider gets certified; your
agency still authorizes its own system,

67
00:04:01.511 --> 00:04:05.010
and never issues a standalone
ATO for a cloud service.

68
00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.485
Here's a telling detail.

69
00:04:06.485 --> 00:04:11.060
Because the rules ship as one machine-readable
file, you can count the words.

70
00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:14.685
The phrase authorization
boundary appears zero times.

71
00:04:14.685 --> 00:04:17.623
The only boundary the rules name is yours.

72
00:04:17.623 --> 00:04:21.298
Mandatory adoption starts January 2027.

73
00:04:21.298 --> 00:04:23.472
So what replaced the provider's boundary?

74
00:04:23.472 --> 00:04:24.600
Scope.

75
00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.712
Scope is now a data question,
not a network diagram.

76
00:04:31.712 --> 00:04:35.000
A resource is in the offering
if it's likely to handle

77
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.976
federal customer data or affect
its security, wherever it sits.

78
00:04:38.976 --> 00:04:43.010
And security category can vary by
resource, which undercuts a lot of

79
00:04:43.010 --> 00:04:46.900
"we can't have that, it would raise
the whole environment" reasoning.

80
00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:50.025
Push back by asking which
resource and which data.

81
00:04:50.025 --> 00:04:53.193
One caution: the agents
and clients you install are

82
00:04:53.193 --> 00:04:56.738
outside FedRAMP altogether,
so they land in your package.

83
00:04:56.738 --> 00:05:01.588
And providers have to publish what's in
scope, though that's services, not features.

84
00:05:01.588 --> 00:05:04.840
Next, how change and evidence now flow.

85
00:05:08.590 --> 00:05:13.103
Under the new rules, providers don't go
back for approval when they change things.

86
00:05:13.103 --> 00:05:18.360
Routine changes need no notice at all,
everyday changes are reported afterward,

87
00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:21.627
and big ones get announced
weeks ahead, all in JSON.

88
00:05:21.627 --> 00:05:24.364
That means you can treat
change notices as data

89
00:05:24.364 --> 00:05:27.240
and test each one against
your own authorization.

90
00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:32.231
Certification evidence moves into
trust centers with programmatic access,

91
00:05:32.231 --> 00:05:36.365
though only a small share of
listings showed one in October 2026.

92
00:05:36.365 --> 00:05:40.615
And for years you'll hold old-style
and new-style packages side by side.

93
00:05:40.615 --> 00:05:42.640
Read each in its own terms.

94
00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:44.310
That's Module 1.

95
00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:52.868
Module 2 gets specific about
Microsoft 365 GCC: what it really is,

96
00:05:52.868 --> 00:05:58.947
what it verifiably loses, and what a High
certification would and wouldn't change.

97
00:05:58.947 --> 00:06:01.346
Then the playbook for
a missing capability,

98
00:06:01.346 --> 00:06:04.970
and the option of changing the
collaboration suite itself.

99
00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:12.870
The common picture of GCC is a
fence around the commercial service.

100
00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:17.895
Microsoft's own documentation describes
something else: the worldwide service,

101
00:06:17.895 --> 00:06:21.795
on the same endpoints, with a list
of specific features left out.

102
00:06:21.795 --> 00:06:24.713
That's good news, because
a list can be read,

103
00:06:24.713 --> 00:06:30.120
argued and worked one line at a time,
while a fence can only be accepted or left.

104
00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:31.908
And the list isn't a ladder.

105
00:06:31.908 --> 00:06:34.921
Some features reach GCC and stop there.

106
00:06:34.921 --> 00:06:37.662
One honest wrinkle: the Marketplace says

107
00:06:37.662 --> 00:06:40.808
GCC rides on Azure
Government infrastructure.

108
00:06:40.808 --> 00:06:43.696
Cite both statements
and paraphrase neither.

109
00:06:43.696 --> 00:06:46.580
So what does GCC actually lose?

110
00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:53.768
The biggest documented loss
isn't a telemetry detail.

111
00:06:53.768 --> 00:06:58.706
It's the whole network connectivity layer
in the admin center: scores per location,

112
00:06:58.706 --> 00:07:01.248
insights like spotting TLS interception,

113
00:07:01.248 --> 00:07:04.880
and attribution of performance
to named network providers.

114
00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:09.455
A commercial agency compares its SASE
and transport options from a dashboard.

115
00:07:09.455 --> 00:07:11.817
A GCC agency gets none of that.

116
00:07:11.817 --> 00:07:13.841
Some measurement survives:

117
00:07:13.841 --> 00:07:18.855
the connectivity test tool still
works in GCC, run from each location.

118
00:07:18.855 --> 00:07:21.118
But the practical message is budget.

119
00:07:21.118 --> 00:07:23.249
If you need comparative network evidence,

120
00:07:23.249 --> 00:07:25.442
you'll be funding the
measurement yourself.

121
00:07:25.442 --> 00:07:28.840
Next, the question
everyone asks about High.

122
00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:37.531
The Marketplace shows three
Microsoft 365 government packages,

123
00:07:37.531 --> 00:07:43.203
and a new one stands out: a package with
the same name as GCC, now pursuing High.

124
00:07:43.203 --> 00:07:44.727
The fact is the listing.

125
00:07:44.727 --> 00:07:48.452
The reading, that it's a High
effort for GCC, is inference,

126
00:07:48.452 --> 00:07:51.928
so ask the provider rather
than planning around it.

127
00:07:51.928 --> 00:07:53.227
Here's the key point.

128
00:07:53.227 --> 00:07:56.416
A higher class changes what data
you can put in the service.

129
00:07:56.416 --> 00:07:58.890
It doesn't bring excluded features back.

130
00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:04.802
Network connectivity is already missing in
GCC High, which is certified High today.

131
00:08:04.802 --> 00:08:06.991
So never wait on a class for a feature.

132
00:08:06.991 --> 00:08:09.100
Next, what to do instead.

133
00:08:12.850 --> 00:08:15.238
Start with a register, not an argument.

134
00:08:15.238 --> 00:08:19.563
One row per feature, each with the
provider's own sentence and a date,

135
00:08:19.563 --> 00:08:23.338
because a citation can't be
dismissed and an inference can.

136
00:08:23.338 --> 00:08:28.241
Then probe your own tenant, and test for
output, not for whether a switch turns on,

137
00:08:28.241 --> 00:08:31.712
because the failure here is
a report that never fills in.

138
00:08:31.712 --> 00:08:34.061
Where there's a gap, build an in-boundary

139
00:08:34.061 --> 00:08:36.775
equivalent and say
plainly that it's derived.

140
00:08:36.775 --> 00:08:41.417
Scheduled network tests from each
location close part of the network gap,

141
00:08:41.417 --> 00:08:43.700
not all of it, and the row should say so.

142
00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:45.738
Some gaps have no equivalent.

143
00:08:45.738 --> 00:08:47.130
That's next.

144
00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:55.155
When there's no equivalent, you take
an exception, and there are two kinds.

145
00:08:55.155 --> 00:09:00.067
The provider's own path, like
ExpressRoute for Microsoft 365, wants a

146
00:09:00.067 --> 00:09:05.417
regulation that mandates it, quoted with
a link, and FedRAMP alone isn't one.

147
00:09:05.417 --> 00:09:09.185
Or your authorizing official
accepts the risk in writing,

148
00:09:09.185 --> 00:09:13.030
with an end date, so permanent
is a decision someone repeats.

149
00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:15.649
The new rules also give
you real arguments,

150
00:09:15.649 --> 00:09:18.642
each with a limit worth
knowing before you use it.

151
00:09:18.642 --> 00:09:21.514
And in acquisitions,
specify the capability,

152
00:09:21.514 --> 00:09:24.929
not a program badge, because
some programs are closed.

153
00:09:24.929 --> 00:09:28.230
Then there's the third
answer: change the service.

154
00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:35.405
Sometimes the honest answer is
a different collaboration suite.

155
00:09:35.405 --> 00:09:38.404
That's only safe if
identity stays where it is:

156
00:09:38.404 --> 00:09:43.430
the suite is a workload that consumes
identity, not a second source of truth.

157
00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:46.178
Read the certification,
not the press release,

158
00:09:46.178 --> 00:09:49.505
because announcements and
listings don't always match.

159
00:09:49.505 --> 00:09:54.418
The public record shows both outcomes:
one department moved fifty thousand people

160
00:09:54.418 --> 00:09:59.118
on a steep temporary discount, and another
dropped licenses almost nobody used.

161
00:09:59.118 --> 00:10:03.883
Keep Entra ID provisioning and federating
sign-in, watch the admin accounts

162
00:10:03.883 --> 00:10:08.043
that skip it, and treat stray edits
in the second directory as drift.

163
00:10:08.043 --> 00:10:09.410
That's Part 1.

164
00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:15.071
Part 2 starts with the landing zone,

165
00:10:15.071 --> 00:10:18.960
where the boundary becomes something
the platform actually enforces.

166
00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:22.646
We'll cover policy, AWS and
GovCloud, the address plan,

167
00:10:22.646 --> 00:10:27.710
network as code, and the front door
every change has to pass through.

168
00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:35.228
If the boundary says a service
is out of scope but the landing

169
00:10:35.228 --> 00:10:39.223
zone lets a team deploy it anyway,
the boundary is just a document.

170
00:10:39.223 --> 00:10:42.772
The management group hierarchy
is where it becomes a control.

171
00:10:42.772 --> 00:10:45.610
Boundary policy goes on
an intermediate root,

172
00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:48.373
and every subscription
beneath inherits it.

173
00:10:48.373 --> 00:10:53.367
Keep the tree flat, separate environments
by subscription so test faces the same

174
00:10:53.367 --> 00:10:56.275
rules as production, and
protect the hierarchy

175
00:10:56.275 --> 00:10:58.884
so new subscriptions
can't land outside it.

176
00:10:58.884 --> 00:11:01.327
One trap for Azure Government teams:

177
00:11:01.327 --> 00:11:05.560
Microsoft's accelerators only
run unmodified in public Azure.

178
00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:08.140
Next, the policies themselves.

179
00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:17.007
Every line in the package's boundary
description should have a mechanism behind it:

180
00:11:17.007 --> 00:11:21.902
which cloud, which regions, which services,
which settings, and which exceptions.

181
00:11:21.902 --> 00:11:23.377
The fine print matters.

182
00:11:23.377 --> 00:11:26.038
The built-in allowed-types
policy skips some

183
00:11:26.038 --> 00:11:29.202
resource types unless you
copy it and change its mode.

184
00:11:29.202 --> 00:11:31.982
And the FedRAMP compliance initiative is,

185
00:11:31.982 --> 00:11:34.841
in Microsoft's own words,
only a partial view.

186
00:11:34.841 --> 00:11:38.240
The rollout is the real
lesson: audit first,

187
00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:43.778
review with the system owner and ISSO,
fix or exempt, and only then deny.

188
00:11:43.778 --> 00:11:47.940
Block production on day one and
teams learn to ask for exemptions.

189
00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:51.140
Next, the same idea on AWS.

190
00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:57.280
AWS gets to the same place with accounts,

191
00:11:57.280 --> 00:12:00.466
organizational units and
service control policies,

192
00:12:00.466 --> 00:12:05.390
but GovCloud works differently enough that
you have to plan for it, not discover it.

193
00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:08.498
Accounts are created in pairs
from the commercial side,

194
00:12:08.498 --> 00:12:11.065
and some factory features
simply aren't there.

195
00:12:11.065 --> 00:12:14.302
The other half of this slide
is about your own automation.

196
00:12:14.302 --> 00:12:17.335
A collector calling a
commercial-only API from

197
00:12:17.335 --> 00:12:20.602
inside the boundary is a
crossing nobody approved.

198
00:12:20.602 --> 00:12:24.766
So every adapter declares its
boundary, resolves endpoints from it,

199
00:12:24.766 --> 00:12:27.964
and fails closed when it
doesn't recognize the cloud.

200
00:12:27.964 --> 00:12:30.080
Next, the address plan.

201
00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:36.155
An inventory needs a denominator.

202
00:12:36.155 --> 00:12:38.686
A scan tells you what
answered during a window;

203
00:12:38.686 --> 00:12:41.217
the address plan tells
you what was handed out.

204
00:12:41.217 --> 00:12:44.718
Reconcile the two, or your
scan is just a sample.

205
00:12:44.718 --> 00:12:47.863
That's why the program
treats IP address management

206
00:12:47.863 --> 00:12:50.643
as the join key everything
else correlates on.

207
00:12:50.643 --> 00:12:55.397
But it isn't the boundary, and it isn't
the asset record: it can't see software,

208
00:12:55.397 --> 00:12:59.993
owners or classification, so a
configuration database carries those.

209
00:12:59.993 --> 00:13:03.767
And because cloud fabrics hand
out addresses with no broadcast,

210
00:13:03.767 --> 00:13:05.904
the plan has to live above all of them.

211
00:13:05.904 --> 00:13:08.330
Next, how Azure mirrors it.

212
00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:16.230
The enterprise address authority
stays in charge, and Azure mirrors it.

213
00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:19.401
Reserve the range in the
enterprise system first,

214
00:13:19.401 --> 00:13:23.269
mirror it into Azure's pools
with outside ranges blocked off,

215
00:13:23.269 --> 00:13:28.142
and use a deny policy so no virtual
network exists without a pool allocation.

216
00:13:28.142 --> 00:13:31.918
Then reconcile, and treat
any mismatch as a finding.

217
00:13:31.918 --> 00:13:34.439
And here's the book's
argument in miniature:

218
00:13:34.439 --> 00:13:38.617
the Azure pool service isn't named
in Microsoft's FedRAMP scope tables.

219
00:13:38.617 --> 00:13:40.843
So until that's confirmed in writing,

220
00:13:40.843 --> 00:13:44.330
record it as a dependency and
choose a path deliberately.

221
00:13:44.330 --> 00:13:47.110
Next, building the network from code.

222
00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:54.795
The network gets built from
reviewed source as five modules,

223
00:13:54.795 --> 00:13:58.888
each owning one plane and depending
only on the layers below it:

224
00:13:58.888 --> 00:14:03.060
platform, address plan, naming,
network, and the enforcement edge.

225
00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:06.200
Naming mechanisms rather
than products is the point,

226
00:14:06.200 --> 00:14:09.910
because changing vendors should
change syntax, not the design.

227
00:14:09.910 --> 00:14:12.335
And some inputs change
underneath your code.

228
00:14:12.335 --> 00:14:17.699
Microsoft's endpoint list is the classic
case, and GCC uses the worldwide list,

229
00:14:17.699 --> 00:14:21.722
so blocking commercial endpoints
would block GCC itself.

230
00:14:21.722 --> 00:14:25.048
Pull that list on a schedule and
apply it through the pipeline.

231
00:14:25.048 --> 00:14:28.500
Which raises the question of
who gets to run that pipeline.

232
00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:37.350
Code that anyone with credentials can apply
is just a click path with extra steps.

233
00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:41.696
It becomes change control
only behind a front door:

234
00:14:41.696 --> 00:14:45.525
request, approve, plan,
apply, reconcile, close.

235
00:14:45.525 --> 00:14:48.242
Each step leaves evidence
for a named control,

236
00:14:48.242 --> 00:14:51.387
and the requester can never
approve their own change.

237
00:14:51.387 --> 00:14:54.513
The step that carries the
most weight is reconcile.

238
00:14:54.513 --> 00:14:57.593
An address block the code
allocated but nobody

239
00:14:57.593 --> 00:15:00.912
recorded is shadow inventory
the moment it exists.

240
00:15:00.912 --> 00:15:03.765
So the pipeline writes
the allocation back,

241
00:15:03.765 --> 00:15:08.125
with the request number and owner,
and fails the run on any mismatch.

242
00:15:08.125 --> 00:15:10.130
Next, how to build it.

243
00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:18.290
There are documented ways to build
this loop on ServiceNow and Terraform,

244
00:15:18.290 --> 00:15:22.917
and whichever you choose, the approver
has to see the actual plan that will run.

245
00:15:22.917 --> 00:15:26.831
The bigger point is that the pipeline
itself is inside the boundary.

246
00:15:26.831 --> 00:15:31.125
Terraform state and plans hold
secrets, even ones marked sensitive,

247
00:15:31.125 --> 00:15:34.193
so where the runner, the
state and the repository

248
00:15:34.193 --> 00:15:37.568
live is a boundary decision,
not a tooling preference.

249
00:15:37.568 --> 00:15:42.268
The program's default is a self-hosted
runner and state inside the boundary.

250
00:15:42.268 --> 00:15:45.843
And after apply, drift goes back
through the same front door.

251
00:15:45.843 --> 00:15:47.590
That's Module 3.

252
00:15:51.340 --> 00:15:55.570
Module 4 is about people and paper:
who decides each boundary question,

253
00:15:55.570 --> 00:15:59.873
and how the boundary gets written
once into the authorization package,

254
00:15:59.873 --> 00:16:04.020
kept current, and backed by the
right questions to the provider.

255
00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:10.658
Most boundary failures
aren't engineering errors.

256
00:16:10.658 --> 00:16:15.490
They're decisions nobody owned: a feature
designed in before anyone checked it,

257
00:16:15.490 --> 00:16:20.396
a workload placed in an unrecorded
subscription, a provider notice nobody read.

258
00:16:20.396 --> 00:16:22.195
So name one decider per row.

259
00:16:22.195 --> 00:16:26.658
The authorizing official decides the
boundary, and that can't be delegated.

260
00:16:26.658 --> 00:16:31.249
The system owner decides placement, not
the platform team, because vending an

261
00:16:31.249 --> 00:16:33.975
unrecorded subscription
is the cheapest way

262
00:16:33.975 --> 00:16:36.558
to make an authorized system unauthorized.

263
00:16:36.558 --> 00:16:39.461
And acquisition matters
more than people expect:

264
00:16:39.461 --> 00:16:43.357
the SKU you buy decides whether
the certification even covers you.

265
00:16:43.357 --> 00:16:45.170
Next, the record.

266
00:16:48.920 --> 00:16:51.920
Your agency never
authorizes a cloud service.

267
00:16:51.920 --> 00:16:55.045
It authorizes its own
system that uses one.

268
00:16:55.045 --> 00:16:58.584
So your package covers only
what you implement, configure,

269
00:16:58.584 --> 00:17:03.082
operate or inherit, and you never paste
the provider's control text into it.

270
00:17:03.082 --> 00:17:07.235
The program's answer is a single
boundary record: what's in scope,

271
00:17:07.235 --> 00:17:11.546
what the probe found, the gaps,
the equivalents and the exceptions.

272
00:17:11.546 --> 00:17:14.970
Everything else cites it, so
when a capability changes,

273
00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:18.395
one record changes and every
narrative stays current.

274
00:17:18.395 --> 00:17:21.520
Information exchanges are
where most crossings live.

275
00:17:21.520 --> 00:17:24.090
Next, how to write the narratives.

276
00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:30.390
Most controls aren't
touched by the boundary.

277
00:17:30.390 --> 00:17:32.851
The ones that are fall into four patterns,

278
00:17:32.851 --> 00:17:35.890
and naming the pattern lets
an assessor follow you.

279
00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:37.678
The hard one is compensating.

280
00:17:37.678 --> 00:17:40.355
Don't describe the missing
feature as if you had it,

281
00:17:40.355 --> 00:17:42.315
and don't hide the substitute either.

282
00:17:42.315 --> 00:17:46.211
Say what the control needs, what's
unavailable with the citation,

283
00:17:46.211 --> 00:17:50.990
what you run instead and what it misses,
and where the remaining gap is recorded.

284
00:17:50.990 --> 00:17:55.847
Also know whose package you're reading:
GCC inherits from Azure Government,

285
00:17:55.847 --> 00:17:59.215
so your chain runs through
two packages, plus the SKU.

286
00:17:59.215 --> 00:18:01.380
Next, keeping it current.

287
00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:08.409
A package describes the system
on the day it was signed,

288
00:18:08.409 --> 00:18:10.230
and the boundary keeps moving.

289
00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:13.856
Three inputs feed one review:
provider change notices,

290
00:18:13.856 --> 00:18:18.530
ongoing certification reports, and
your own probe and drift findings.

291
00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:22.074
Each gets one question: does
this change a disposition,

292
00:18:22.074 --> 00:18:24.043
an equivalent or an exception?

293
00:18:24.043 --> 00:18:27.704
If it might change your risk, it
goes to the authorizing official,

294
00:18:27.704 --> 00:18:30.329
who decides whether it's
a significant change.

295
00:18:30.329 --> 00:18:34.855
A provider removing a capability your
narrative depends on is exactly that.

296
00:18:34.855 --> 00:18:37.630
The table is what an assessor
should be able to find.

297
00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:40.080
Next, what to ask providers.

298
00:18:43.830 --> 00:18:46.567
Asking for commercial
parity can't be answered.

299
00:18:46.567 --> 00:18:50.793
A specific question tied to a
published rule or exclusion can.

300
00:18:50.793 --> 00:18:55.705
So name the item, cite the basis, and
ask for data rather than documents.

301
00:18:55.705 --> 00:18:58.873
The eight requests on the
slide follow that pattern,

302
00:18:58.873 --> 00:19:03.661
and most of them rest on obligations the
2026 rules already place on providers,

303
00:19:03.661 --> 00:19:07.492
like trust center access and
machine-readable change notices.

304
00:19:07.492 --> 00:19:11.617
Microsoft's own exception process
sets the standard you can hold it to.

305
00:19:11.617 --> 00:19:15.887
Record every request, its date
and the answer in your package,

306
00:19:15.887 --> 00:19:18.705
because a documented no is evidence too.

307
00:19:18.705 --> 00:19:20.780
Let's close with decisions.

308
00:19:24.530 --> 00:19:28.293
Six decisions, each with an
owner, and two matter most.

309
00:19:28.293 --> 00:19:32.639
The authorizing official settles the
boundary before workloads arrive,

310
00:19:32.639 --> 00:19:35.204
because discovering it later is expensive.

311
00:19:35.204 --> 00:19:38.680
And every gap gets a
deliberate response, recorded.

312
00:19:38.680 --> 00:19:42.830
You can start this week without buying
anything: re-cite your gap list,

313
00:19:42.830 --> 00:19:45.960
read each service's Marketplace
class with the date,

314
00:19:45.960 --> 00:19:50.255
put allowed regions in audit, and name
someone to read provider notices.

315
00:19:50.255 --> 00:19:54.255
The license says what you bought;
the boundary says what you may use.

316
00:19:54.255 --> 00:19:57.543
Write it down once, cite it
everywhere, keep it current.

317
00:19:57.543 --> 00:20:00.830
The book has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

318
00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:01.660
Thank you.
